<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  LOOP RECORDING STARTED PARTWAY INTO THE CYCLE: the cycle region is the TAKE
  region, and every pass is one take OF IT.

  Env, set on the CTest entry: SMARAGD_AUDIO_INPUT_BACKEND=file:../test_position.wav,
  SMARAGD_AUDIO_INPUT_LATENCY_FRAMES=4800, SMARAGD_CAPTURE_SPEED=1; RUN_SERIAL.

  WHAT THIS CASE EXISTS FOR, and why `record_loop_takes` could not catch it.
  That case records from the LOOP START, where every pass is a whole one and
  the wrapped position of every captured frame equals its unwrapped position
  modulo nothing interesting. Starting PARTWAY IN is the shape that broke:
  the recorder chopped the performance at each wrap and placed each piece at
  its own wrapped position, so pieces landing on the same spot became "takes"
  that were really CONSECUTIVE FRAGMENTS OF ONE PERFORMANCE, and the middle of
  the performance landed as a PLAIN CLIP that could not be comped at all.

  Reported from a real session  -  a 4-bar cycle, recording begun in its last
  bar, "takes only in the 4th of four bars, one time from the beginning plus
  the tail"  -  and reproduced here headlessly at 1/4 scale: a 2 s cycle, the
  record start at 1.5 s, five seconds sung.

  THE NUMBERS. The locator is 72000 and the compensation is the same -5824
  `record_offset_zero` derives, so capture frame 0 maps to project frame
  66176  -  29824 frames short of the wrap. Pass 1 is therefore a LATE pass and
  its take must open with 66176 frames of nothing, which is a NEGATIVE source
  offset, i.e. leading silence (proposal 23). Pass 2 is a whole one and covers
  the region from its first frame.

  WHY THE TAKE COUNT IS A FLOOR. Five seconds over a 2 s cycle is 2.5 passes,
  plus the partial one at each end  -  and how much material a take actually
  captures is a WALL-CLOCK quantity that shrinks under load (`record_loop_takes`
  failed exactly that way before it was relaxed). So the count is a floor, and
  the part that is not load-sensitive is asserted instead: ONE COLUMN, spanning
  EXACTLY the cycle region, with as many TAKES as there were PASSES (which
  `assert-recorded-clip` checks unasked).

  The two energy pairs are the audible half and they are what distinguishes
  the fix from a cosmetic re-labelling: take 0 (the late pass) must be SILENT
  over the region the performer had not started singing yet and loud after it,
  while take 1 (a whole pass) must be loud in that same early region.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="record_loop_late_start">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="0" mode="off"/>

    <!-- NAME THE INPUT DEVICE, so `audio/recordingOffsetMs/<device>` is read
         under a name no real device can have rather than under whatever this
         machine's own calibration is filed as. See record_offset_zero. -->
    <set-track-input trackPath="0" input="audio:qxa-record-input:1"/>

    <!-- The cycle region: 0 .. 2 s. set-property takes a JSON ARRAY. -->
    <set-property key="rangeStart" value="[0]"/>
    <set-property key="rangeEnd" value="[96000]"/>
    <set-property key="rangeValid" value="[true]"/>
    <cycle-enable/>

    <!-- THE POINT OF THE CASE: start recording in the LAST QUARTER of the
         cycle, not at its start. -->
    <set-locator position="72000"/>
    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="1"/>

    <record-start/>
    <wait-ms ms="5000"/>
    <record-stop/>

    <!-- ONE column, at the CYCLE START and exactly the cycle long, whatever
         the pass the recording began in. Pre-fix this read TWO clips - a
         column in the last quarter and an uncompable plain cut before it. -->
    <assert-recorded-clip trackPath="0" clips="1" minTakes="2" minPasses="2"
                          startFrame="0" startTolerance="0"
                          durationFrames="96000" durationTolerance="0"
                          inputLatencyFrames="4800"
                          outputLatencyFrames="1024"
                          compensationFrames="-5824"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct"/>

    <!-- THE AUDIBLE HALF. Take 0 is pass 1, which began 29824 frames before
         the wrap: silent over the first second, singing in the last quarter.
         The windows stay well clear of 66176 on both sides so the assertion
         is about the pass boundary, not about the frame the trim landed on. -->
    <select-take clip="0,0" take="0"/>
    <render filename="rlls_take0.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="2.0"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="rlls_take0.wav" startFrame="4000"
                         frameCount="56000" channel="0"
                         minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.0005"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="rlls_take0.wav" startFrame="76000"
                         frameCount="16000" channel="0"
                         minRms="0.02" maxRms="0.95"/>

    <!-- Take 1 is a WHOLE pass and carries material over the same early
         window take 0 is silent in. Without this the case would pass on an
         implementation that simply placed silence everywhere. -->
    <select-take clip="0,0" take="1"/>
    <render filename="rlls_take1.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="2.0"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="rlls_take1.wav" startFrame="4000"
                         frameCount="56000" channel="0"
                         minRms="0.02" maxRms="0.95"/>

    <assert-render-policy liveThreadRefusals="0" liveOwnedRefusals="0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
